# meta::salientEntities = 7 (5*), 78 (5*), 100 (5*), 166 (5*), 3 (4*), 6 (3), 54 (3*), 51 (2), 109 (2*), 119 (2*), 2 (1*), 14 (1*), 41 (1*), 61 (1*), 62 (1*), 65 (1), 66 (1), 74 (1), 77 (1), 80 (1*), 81 (1*), 87 (1), 90 (1*), 103 (1*), 122 (1), 129 (1), 152 (1), 153 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Oakland
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This guide to Oakland describes the city's history since the 1960s and its neighborhoods, including Downtown and its skyline, Chinatown's architecture and bilingual signs, Uptown with its art galleries, theaters and night clubs, North Oakland's commercial centers, foodie heavens, Temescal and Rockridge, and working-class West Oakland.
# meta::summary2 = (human2) Oakland, a diverse Californian city known for its radical political culture located East of San Francisco, includes a historic downtown, an artistic uptown, a suburban Northern district, and a working-class Western district.
# meta::summary3 = (human3) Oakland, which is separated into Old Oakland, Uptown, North Oakland, and West Oakland, is a city near San Francisco remarkable for being a hub of radical culture, having a diverse population, and having a wide-reaching public transit system.
# meta::summary4 = (human4) Oakland, California is a multicultural city in the Bay Area with a number of diverse neighborhoods, including the city center Downtown, the culturally rich Chinatown, the artistic and lively Uptown, North Oakland, which boasts a wide variety of delicious food, and West Oakland, a working class neighborhood.
# meta::summary5 = (human5) The guide describes Oakland, a radical culture hub and multicultural "hipster" city neighboring San Francisco in the Bay Area, including its following neighborhoods and some notable landmarks: Downtown, Old Oakland, Chinatown, Jack London Square, USS Potomac, Financial District/Uptown, North Oakland, Temescal, Rockridge, Piedmont Avenue, Grand Lake, Lakeshore, and West Oakland.
